# TypeScript narrowing at the boundary

Validate unknown input once, then pass a useful domain type deeper into the application. Keep unsafe checks near the boundary instead of spreading them across every function.

```ts
type User = { id: string; displayName: string };

function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return Boolean(value && typeof value === "object" && "id" in value && typeof value.id === "string");
}
```

## Review questions

- Is the runtime check stricter than the TypeScript type?
- Can an invalid value cross the module boundary?
- Does the error identify the rejected field without logging sensitive data?

# Node.js event loop

JavaScript runs callbacks on a single main thread, while asynchronous I/O can progress outside that call stack. The important question is when a continuation becomes eligible to run.

```js
console.log("sync");
setTimeout(() => console.log("timer"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("microtask"));
console.log("after");
```

Expected order: `sync`, `after`, `microtask`, then `timer`. Long synchronous work still blocks every callback, so moving a function behind a Promise does not make the work cheap.

> [!NOTE]
> Measure event-loop delay before blaming the database for every latency spike.

# Debounce and throttle

Use **debounce** when the final value matters, such as search after typing. Use **throttle** when updates should continue at a bounded rate, such as scroll position or pointer movement.

```js
function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
  };
}
```

## Questions before adding one

- Does the operation need cancellation?
- Should the last call always run?
- What happens when the component or page closes?
- Is the delay hiding a slow backend rather than reducing work?

# Queue consumers

A worker should not accept unlimited work just because the queue can hold it. Bound concurrency, acknowledge only after the durable side effect, and make retries observable.

```js
for await (const job of queue.consume({ concurrency: 4 })) {
  await processJob(job);
  await job.ack();
}
```

## Failure questions

- Is the handler idempotent when the worker crashes after the side effect?
- Does one poison message block unrelated work?
- Is retry delay long enough to avoid a hot loop?
- What metric tells us the queue is falling behind?

# Input validation

Validation has two jobs: protect invariants and give the caller a useful correction path. Normalize harmless differences once, then validate the normalized value at the boundary.

```js
function normalizeTitle(value) {
  if (typeof value !== "string") throw new TypeError("Title must be text.");
  const title = value.trim().replace(/\s+/g, " " );
  if (!title) throw new Error("Title is required.");
  if (title.length > 160) throw new Error("Title is too long.");
  return title;
}
```

Do not silently truncate user content. Keep the limit close to the data contract and test empty, oversized, malformed, and valid input separately.

# Node.js streams

Streams let a program process data incrementally instead of holding the full payload in memory.

```js
for await (const chunk of readable) {
  await handle(chunk);
}
```

Remember to discuss backpressure, error propagation, and cancellation in an interview answer.
